---

<Layout
  title={`Control and Coordination | CBSE Class 10 Science (Biology) | ${provider.name}`}
  description="In-depth revision notes, neural pathways, phytohormones, endocrine glands, interactive SVG diagrams (Reflex Arc & Brain Architecture), and 20 CBSE Board MCQs for Control and Coordination."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Control and Coordination
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Science (Biology) · Chapter 6/7</span>
      <h1>Control and Coordination: Neural, Endocrine &amp; Plant Systems</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Master nervous signaling, reflex arcs, brain functional anatomy, phytohormones (auxins to ABA), and human endocrine feedback loops with 20 board exam practice questions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>1. The Nervous System &amp; Reflex Action</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚡ Structure of a Neuron &amp; Synaptic Transmission</h3>
          <p class="formula">Dendrite &rarr; Cell Body (Cyton) &rarr; Axon &rarr; Synapse (Neurotransmitter)</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Dendritic Tip:</strong> Specialized receptor ends acquire information from the environment, initiating an electrical impulse.</li>
            <li><strong>Axon Transmission:</strong> The electrical impulse travels along the long axonal projection insulated by myelin sheaths to reach the nerve ending.</li>
            <li><strong>The Synapse:</strong> The microscopic gap between the axon terminal of one neuron and the dendrite of the next. Electrical impulses cannot jump this physical gap; they trigger the release of chemical messengers (neurotransmitters like acetylcholine) that diffuse across the synapse to initiate a fresh electrical impulse in the next neuron.</li>
            <li><strong>Directionality:</strong> Chemical synapses ensure impulses travel in one direction only.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚡ Reflex Arc &amp; Instinctive Action</h3>
          <p class="formula">Receptor &rarr; Sensory Neuron &rarr; Spinal Cord (Relay Neuron) &rarr; Motor Neuron &rarr; Effector</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Definition:</strong> An involuntary, instantaneous unconscious response to a dangerous external stimulus (e.g., pulling your hand away immediately upon touching a hot flame).</li>
            <li><strong>Why Reflex Arcs Evolved:</strong> The thinking tissue of the brain is complex and slow. Reflex arcs evolved in animals as an ultra-rapid survival mechanism directly mediated by the spinal cord without waiting for cerebral analysis.</li>
            <li><strong>Five Components:</strong>
              <br/>1. <em>Receptor:</em> Detects stimulus (heat receptors in skin).
              <br/>2. <em>Sensory Neuron:</em> Carries signal towards spinal cord.
              <br/>3. <em>Relay Neuron:</em> Processes and redirects signal in spinal cord gray matter.
              <br/>4. <em>Motor Neuron:</em> Transmits response command to muscle.
              <br/>5. <em>Effector:</em> Biceps muscle contracts to withdraw limb.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Brain & Plant Coordination -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>2. Human Brain &amp; Plant Tropisms</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🧠 Human Brain: Functional Architecture</h3>
          <p class="formula">Cranium (Skull) + 3 Meninges + Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF) Shock Absorber</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Forebrain (Cerebrum):</strong> Main thinking part. Has distinct sensory areas for sight, hearing, smell; association areas for memory and reasoning; motor areas for voluntary muscle movements; and the central hunger regulation center.</li>
            <li><strong>Midbrain:</strong> Controls involuntary visual and auditory reflex movements of the head, neck, and trunk.</li>
            <li><strong>Hindbrain:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Cerebellum:</em> Coordinates precision of voluntary actions and maintains posture, balance, and equilibrium (e.g., walking in a straight line, riding a bicycle, picking up a pen).
              <br/>• <em>Medulla Oblongata:</em> Controls crucial involuntary survival functions: blood pressure, salivation, heartbeat, swallowing, and vomiting.
              <br/>• <em>Pons:</em> Assists in the pneumatic regulation of breathing rate.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌱 Plant Coordination: Hormones &amp; Movements</h3>
          <p class="formula">Growth Promoters (Auxin, GA, Cytokinin) vs. Growth Inhibitor (ABA)</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Phytohormones:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Auxin:</em> Synthesized at shoot tips; diffuses to shaded side, causing cells there to grow longer so the shoot bends towards light (Phototropism).
              <br/>• <em>Gibberellins:</em> Promotes longitudinal stem growth and seed germination.
              <br/>• <em>Cytokinins:</em> Promotes rapid cell division; concentrated in fruits and seeds.
              <br/>• <em>Abscisic Acid (ABA):</em> Growth inhibitor; signals stomatal closure during water stress and causes wilting/falling of leaves.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Tropic Movements (Directional Growth):</strong> Phototropism (light), Geotropism (gravity; roots down, shoots up), Hydrotropism (water), Chemotropism (growth of pollen tube towards ovule).</li>
            <li><strong>Nastic Movements (Non-directional):</strong> E.g., <em>Mimosa pudica</em> (Touch-me-not) leaves droop immediately upon touch due to rapid osmotic water loss in pulvinus cells (no cell growth involved).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Endocrine System Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>3. Endocrine Glands &amp; Hormonal Feedback Loops</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>💉 Human Endocrine Glands &amp; Master Secretions</h3>
          <p class="formula">Ductless Glands &rarr; Direct Secretion into Bloodstream &rarr; Target Organs</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Pituitary Gland (Master Gland):</strong> Secretes Growth Hormone (GH). Deficiency in childhood causes dwarfism; excess secretion causes gigantism.</li>
            <li><strong>Thyroid Gland:</strong> Secretes Thyroxine, regulating carbohydrate, protein, and fat metabolism. <em>Iodine is essential</em> for thyroxine synthesis; dietary deficiency leads to Goitre (enlarged neck).</li>
            <li><strong>Pancreas:</strong> Secretes Insulin from Islets of Langerhans. Regulates and lowers blood glucose levels; under-secretion causes Diabetes Mellitus (treated with insulin injections).</li>
            <li><strong>Adrenal Gland:</strong> Secretes Adrenaline (the "fight-or-flight" hormone). Prepares the body for emergency by increasing heart rate, dilating bronchioles, and shunting blood from skin/digestive organs to skeletal muscles.</li>
            <li><strong>Testes &amp; Ovaries:</strong> Secrete Testosterone (male) and Estrogen/Progesterone (female), driving puberty changes and gametogenesis.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🔄 The Hormonal Feedback Mechanism</h3>
          <p class="formula">Homeostasis · Self-Regulating Negative Feedback Loop</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Why Necessary?</strong> Hormones must be released in exact, minute quantities at specific times. Excess or deficiency causes severe clinical pathology.</li>
            <li><strong>Blood Sugar Example:</strong>
              <br/>1. When blood sugar levels rise after a meal, pancreatic beta-cells detect the spike.
              <br/>2. Pancreas responds by secreting more insulin into the blood.
              <br/>3. Insulin causes muscle and liver cells to take up glucose and convert it into glycogen.
              <br/>4. As blood sugar drops back to baseline normal, the stimulus ceases, and insulin secretion automatically shuts down.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: Neural Circuits &amp; Cerebral Architecture</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <article class="card visual-card">
          <span class="visual-badge">Neural Circuitry</span>
          <h3>The Reflex Arc Circuit</h3>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 600 320" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
              <rect width="600" height="320" fill="#f8fafc" />
              
              <!-- Stimulus & Receptor (Left) -->
              <circle cx="60" cy="80" r="30" fill="#fee2e2" stroke="#dc2626" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="60" y="85" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#991b1b">🔥 Heat</text>
              <text x="60" y="130" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#7f1d1d">Skin Receptor</text>

              <!-- Sensory Neuron (Blue Arc) -->
              <path d="M 90 80 Q 220 30 350 80" fill="none" stroke="#0284c7" stroke-width="3" />
              <text x="220" y="50" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#0369a1">Sensory Neuron &rarr;</text>

              <!-- Spinal Cord Cross Section (Right) -->
              <rect x="350" y="50" width="220" height="180" rx="12" fill="#fff" stroke="#475569" stroke-width="2" />
              <path d="M 420 100 Q 460 70 500 100 Q 460 140 420 100 Z" fill="#e2e8f0" stroke="#94a3b8" />
              <text x="460" y="35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="700" fill="#334155">SPINAL CORD</text>

              <!-- Relay Neuron -->
              <line x1="380" y1="90" x2="420" y2="150" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="4" />
              <text x="440" y="160" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#166534">Relay Neuron</text>

              <!-- Message to Brain -->
              <path d="M 460 80 L 460 20" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="3" />
              <text x="470" y="25" font-size="9" fill="#64748b">To Brain</text>

              <!-- Motor Neuron (Red Arc back) -->
              <path d="M 420 150 Q 240 220 120 220" fill="none" stroke="#dc2626" stroke-width="3" />
              <text x="240" y="240" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#dc2626">&larr; Motor Neuron</text>

              <!-- Effector (Arm Muscle) -->
              <rect x="40" y="200" width="80" height="50" rx="8" fill="#dcfce7" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="80" y="225" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#166534">Biceps</text>
              <text x="80" y="240" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#14532d">(Effector)</text>

              <text x="80" y="280" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#15803d">&rarr; Arm Withdraws!</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">Figure 1: The spinal reflex arc bypasses conscious brain processing to achieve instantaneous physical withdrawal.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="card visual-card">
          <span class="visual-badge">Neuro-Anatomy</span>
          <h3>Human Brain: Three Major Regions</h3>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 600 320" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
              <rect width="600" height="320" fill="#f8fafc" />
              
              <!-- Forebrain (Cerebrum) -->
              <path d="M 120 140 C 100 40, 360 20, 440 90 C 480 130, 420 180, 360 170 C 260 180, 160 180, 120 140 Z" fill="#e0f2fe" stroke="#0284c7" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="260" y="100" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="#0369a1">CEREBRUM (Forebrain)</text>
              <text x="260" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="#0c4a6e">Thinking, Memory, Voluntary Action, Senses, Hunger</text>

              <!-- Midbrain -->
              <circle cx="320" cy="180" r="22" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="320" y="185" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="700" fill="#92400e">Midbrain</text>

              <!-- Hindbrain Components -->
              <!-- Cerebellum -->
              <ellipse cx="440" cy="200" rx="45" ry="30" fill="#dcfce7" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="440" y="200" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#166534">Cerebellum</text>
              <text x="440" y="215" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#14532d">Balance &amp; Posture</text>

              <!-- Pons & Medulla -->
              <rect x="300" y="205" width="40" height="30" rx="4" fill="#fae8ff" stroke="#a855f7" stroke-width="1.5" />
              <text x="320" y="225" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="700" fill="#7e22ce">Pons</text>

              <rect x="300" y="240" width="40" height="40" rx="4" fill="#fee2e2" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="1.5" />
              <text x="320" y="260" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" font-weight="700" fill="#991b1b">Medulla</text>

              <!-- Spinal Cord Trunk -->
              <rect x="310" y="280" width="20" height="35" fill="#cbd5e1" />
              <text x="360" y="300" font-size="10" font-weight="700" fill="#475569">&rarr; Spinal Cord</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">Figure 2: The tripartite human brain integrating conscious cognition (forebrain), reflex optics (midbrain), and autonomic balance (hindbrain).</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Quiz Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="quiz-header">
        <div>
          <h2>CBSE Board Examination Practice: Control &amp; Coordination</h2>
          <p class="muted">20 High-yield questions testing neuron polarity, brain divisions, phytohormone functions, and endocrine pathologies.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="quiz-score-badge" id="quizScoreBadge">Score: 0 / 20</div>
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-container" id="quizApp"></div>

      <div class="quiz-footer-actions">
        <span class="muted" id="quizProgressText">0 of 20 answered</span>
        <button class="button secondary" id="resetQuizBtn">Reset Quiz</button>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA Section -->
    <section class="cta-banner">
      <h2>Master Complex Biological Mechanisms with 1-on-1 Guidance</h2>
      <p>Clear every doubt on neurobiology, endocrinology, and plant tropisms with personalized board coaching.</p>
      <a class="button" href={calBookingUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Book Your Free Trial Class</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
  const questions = [
    {
      q: "In a neuron, through which structural component does environmental information initially enter as an electrical signal?",
      opts: [
        "Axon terminal",
        "Dendritic tip",
        "Myelin sheath",
        "Synaptic vesicle"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Information in a nerve cell is acquired at the dendritic tip, which sets off an electrical impulse traveling down the cyton and axon."
    },
    {
      q: "Why can nerve impulses cross a chemical synapse in only one direction?",
      opts: [
        "Because dendrites possess electrical gates while axons do not",
        "Because neurotransmitter chemical vesicles are present only in axon terminals, and corresponding receptors exist on the post-synaptic dendritic membrane",
        "Because the myelin sheath blocks backward flow",
        "Because the spinal cord commands unidirectional gravity flow"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Neurotransmitter chemicals are stored and released solely from pre-synaptic axon terminals, diffusing across the synaptic cleft to bind receptors on the post-synaptic dendrite."
    },
    {
      q: "Which specific region of the human brain is responsible for maintaining posture, equilibrium, and precision of voluntary movements like riding a bicycle?",
      opts: [
        "Cerebrum",
        "Medulla Oblongata",
        "Cerebellum",
        "Hypothalamus"
      ],
      correct: 2,
      exp: "The cerebellum (part of the hindbrain) coordinates voluntary muscle precision and bodily balance and posture."
    },
    {
      q: "Which part of the brain regulates essential involuntary functions such as blood pressure, salivation, and vomiting?",
      opts: [
        "Cerebrum in Forebrain",
        "Medulla in Hindbrain",
        "Midbrain",
        "Pons"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Involuntary actions including arterial blood pressure, salivation, swallowing, and peristalsis are controlled by the medulla oblongata in the hindbrain."
    },
    {
      q: "What protective structures safeguard the human brain against mechanical injuries and shocks?",
      opts: [
        "Only the thick hair on the scalp",
        "The bony cranium (skull), three protective meningeal membranes, and shock-absorbing cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)",
        "The clavicle bone and thyroid cartilage",
        "A dense layer of subcutaneous fat"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "The brain sits inside the bony cranium, surrounded by three meninges and floating in cerebrospinal fluid, which acts as a shock absorber."
    },
    {
      q: "How does the plant growth hormone Auxin cause a shoot to bend towards a unidirectional light source (phototropism)?",
      opts: [
        "Auxin evaporates on the illuminated side",
        "Auxin diffuses towards the shaded side of the shoot, stimulating cells on the darker side to elongate faster than those on the illuminated side",
        "Auxin destroys chlorophyll on the sunny side",
        "Auxin attracts gravity towards the light"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Auxin synthesized at the shoot tip moves to the shaded side; the higher auxin concentration causes shaded cells to elongate more, bending the shoot toward light."
    },
    {
      q: "Which phytohormone acts as a growth inhibitor, promoting the wilting and falling of mature leaves during dry stress?",
      opts: [
        "Auxin",
        "Gibberellin",
        "Cytokinin",
        "Abscisic Acid (ABA)"
      ],
      correct: 3,
      exp: "Abscisic Acid (ABA) is a stress hormone that inhibits plant growth, triggers stomatal closure to conserve water, and induces leaf fall (abscission)."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the primary mechanism causing the leaves of the sensitive plant (Mimosa pudica) to fold and droop rapidly upon being touched?",
      opts: [
        "Directional cell division towards the stem",
        "Rapid change in the turgor pressure of water inside pulvinar cells at the leaf bases",
        "Secretions of plant digestive enzymes",
        "Electrical contraction of muscular proteins"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "The nastic movement of Mimosa pudica is non-growth; touch triggers electrical-chemical signals causing sudden water loss from cells in the pulvinus, resulting in drooping."
    },
    {
      q: "The growth of a pollen tube through the style towards the ovarian ovule is a classic biological demonstration of which tropism?",
      opts: [
        "Phototropism",
        "Chemotropism",
        "Geotropism",
        "Thigmotropism"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "The growth of the pollen tube towards the ovule is guided by chemical attractants secreted by the ovary, illustrating chemotropism."
    },
    {
      q: "Why is the use of iodised salt strongly recommended in our daily diet?",
      opts: [
        "Iodine prevents dental cavities",
        "Iodine is essential for the thyroid gland to synthesize thyroxine hormone, which regulates carbohydrate, protein, and fat metabolism and prevents goitre",
        "Iodine stimulates insulin secretion in the pancreas",
        "Iodine increases hemoglobin production in bone marrow"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Iodine is required for the thyroid gland to produce thyroxine. Lack of dietary iodine causes thyroid enlargement (goitre)."
    },
    {
      q: "Which hormone is administered via subcutaneous injections to patients suffering from severe Diabetes Mellitus, and why?",
      opts: [
        "Thyroxine; to speed up metabolic digestion",
        "Insulin; to lower and regulate abnormally high blood glucose levels by enabling cellular uptake",
        "Adrenaline; to increase heartbeat and blood circulation",
        "Growth Hormone; to stimulate kidney filtration"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Insulin produced by pancreatic beta-cells regulates blood sugar; diabetic patients lacking sufficient insulin require injections to prevent hyper-glycemia."
    },
    {
      q: "What physiological changes occur in the human body when adrenaline is secreted in response to a frightening emergency?",
      opts: [
        "Heartbeat slows down and digestion increases",
        "Heart beats faster, breathing rate increases due to rib muscle contraction, and blood is diverted away from digestive organs to skeletal muscles",
        "Pupils constrict and sweat glands stop functioning",
        "Body enters a sleep state to preserve energy"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Adrenaline initiates the fight-or-flight response, boosting cardiac output, respiration, and directing blood flow to skeletal muscles for rapid action."
    },
    {
      q: "Which endocrine gland is commonly designated as the 'Master Gland' of the human body, and where is it located?",
      opts: [
        "Thyroid gland, located in the neck",
        "Pituitary gland, located at the base of the brain",
        "Adrenal gland, located on top of the kidneys",
        "Pancreas, located below the stomach"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "The pituitary gland secretes tropic hormones that control and stimulate other endocrine glands, functioning as the master endocrine gland."
    },
    {
      q: "How does the negative feedback mechanism regulate insulin secretion when blood glucose levels fluctuate?",
      opts: [
        "Insulin is secreted continuously in equal amounts 24 hours a day",
        "High blood sugar triggers pancreas cells to secrete insulin; as blood sugar falls to normal, insulin secretion is automatically shut off",
        "Brain sends an electrical nerve shock to the liver",
        "The stomach produces enzymes that destroy excess insulin"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Pancreatic islet cells detect blood glucose levels directly; when levels drop to normal, the stimulus for insulin secretion ceases."
    },
    {
      q: "Which of the following is an involuntary action, yet NOT a reflex action?",
      opts: [
        "Blinking when dust blows into the eyes",
        "Withdrawing the foot when stepping on a sharp nail",
        "Regular beating of the heart and peristaltic movement of the gut",
        "Sneezing when pepper enters the nostrils"
      ],
      correct: 2,
      exp: "Heartbeat and peristalsis are continuous autonomic involuntary actions governed by the medulla, whereas reflex actions are immediate protective responses to specific external stimuli."
    },
    {
      q: "What causes dwarfism in human children?",
      opts: [
        "Deficiency of thyroxine in infancy",
        "Deficiency of growth hormone secreted by the pituitary gland during childhood",
        "Excess secretion of adrenaline",
        "Lack of calcium in the liver"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Growth hormone (GH) secreted by the anterior pituitary regulates longitudinal bone and somatic growth; childhood deficiency results in dwarfism."
    },
    {
      q: "Which plant hormone is present in highest concentrations in areas of rapid cell division, such as developing fruits and seeds?",
      opts: [
        "Cytokinin",
        "Abscisic Acid",
        "Ethylene gas",
        "Auxin"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      exp: "Cytokinins promote cytokinesis (cell division) and are naturally concentrated in rapidly growing tissues like young fruits, seeds, and shoot buds."
    },
    {
      q: "What is the correct pathway followed by a nerve impulse in a spinal reflex arc?",
      opts: [
        "Effector &rarr; Motor neuron &rarr; Spinal cord &rarr; Sensory neuron &rarr; Receptor",
        "Receptor &rarr; Sensory neuron &rarr; Relay neuron in spinal cord &rarr; Motor neuron &rarr; Effector muscle",
        "Receptor &rarr; Brain &rarr; Motor neuron &rarr; Sensory neuron &rarr; Effector",
        "Sensory neuron &rarr; Receptor &rarr; Effector &rarr; Motor neuron"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "The reflex arc sequence is: Receptor detects &rarr; Sensory neuron conducts to spinal cord &rarr; Relay neuron integrates &rarr; Motor neuron commands &rarr; Effector executes."
    },
    {
      q: "Which hormone is responsible for the dramatic physical changes associated with puberty in teenage boys?",
      opts: [
        "Estrogen",
        "Thyroxine",
        "Testosterone secreted by the testes",
        "Insulin"
      ],
      correct: 2,
      exp: "Testosterone secreted by the interstitial Leydig cells of the testes stimulates male secondary sexual characteristics (deep voice, facial hair, muscle mass)."
    },
    {
      q: "Why do roots exhibit positive geotropism while plant shoots exhibit negative geotropism?",
      opts: [
        "Because roots dislike sunlight",
        "Roots grow downward in the direction of the Earth's gravitational pull to anchor and find water, whereas shoots grow upward against gravity towards light",
        "Because earthworms pull root tips down",
        "Because gravity only exists in the soil"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      exp: "Downward growth of roots with the gravity vector is positive geotropism; upward growth of stems away from the gravitational pull is negative geotropism."
    }
  ];

  let userAnswers = {};

  function renderQuiz() {
    const container = document.getElementById('quizApp');
    if (!container) return;
    container.innerHTML = '';

    questions.forEach((qItem, idx) => {
      const card = document.createElement('div');
      card.className = 'quiz-card';

      const qTitle = document.createElement('h3');
      qTitle.className = 'quiz-question';
      qTitle.textContent = `${idx + 1}. ${qItem.q}`;
      card.appendChild(qTitle);

      const optsDiv = document.createElement('div');
      optsDiv.className = 'quiz-options';

      qItem.opts.forEach((optText, optIdx) => {
        const btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.className = 'quiz-opt-btn';
        btn.textContent = optText;

        if (userAnswers[idx] !== undefined) {
          btn.disabled = true;
          if (optIdx === qItem.correct) {
            btn.classList.add('opt-correct');
          } else if (userAnswers[idx] === optIdx) {
            btn.classList.add('opt-wrong');
          }
        } else {
          btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
            userAnswers[idx] = optIdx;
            renderQuiz();
            updateScoreSummary();
          });
        }
        optsDiv.appendChild(btn);
      });

      card.appendChild(optsDiv);

      if (userAnswers[idx] !== undefined) {
        const fb = document.createElement('div');
        fb.className = 'quiz-feedback ' + (userAnswers[idx] === qItem.correct ? 'feedback-correct' : 'feedback-wrong');
        fb.innerHTML = (userAnswers[idx] === qItem.correct ? '<strong>✓ Correct!</strong> ' : '<strong>✗ Incorrect.</strong> ') + qItem.exp;
        card.appendChild(fb);
      }

      container.appendChild(card);
    });
  }

  function updateScoreSummary() {
    let score = 0;
    const answeredCount = Object.keys(userAnswers).length;
    for (let qIdx in userAnswers) {
      if (userAnswers[qIdx] === questions[qIdx].correct) {
        score++;
      }
    }
    const badge = document.getElementById('quizScoreBadge');
    if (badge) badge.textContent = `Score: ${score} / ${questions.length}`;

    const prog = document.getElementById('quizProgressText');
    if (prog) prog.textContent = `${answeredCount} of ${questions.length} answered`;
  }

  function resetQuiz() {
    userAnswers = {};
    renderQuiz();
    updateScoreSummary();
  }

  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    renderQuiz();
    const resetBtn = document.getElementById('resetQuizBtn');
    if (resetBtn) resetBtn.addEventListener('click', resetQuiz);
  });
</script>

